WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.151 --> 00:00:09.274
<v Maya>So I want you to imagine the absolute most frustrating paperwork heavy interaction you have ever had with a government agency.</v>

2
00:00:09.535 --> 00:00:10.555
<v Daniel>Oh yeah. We all have one.</v>

3
00:00:10.795 --> 00:00:22.719
<v Maya>Right. You know the exact scenario. You're sitting in this dreary waiting room. You have this massive stack of incredibly dense forms and you are trying to figure out if you need like form 104 B or 104 C.</v>

4
00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:29.384
<v Daniel>And you're just waiting. It always feels like it's deliberately designed to make you just, you know, give up and go home.</v>

5
00:00:29.544 --> 00:00:38.030
<v Maya>Exactly. It's miserable. But now I want you to imagine that you don't have to do any of that ever again. You just have an AI assistant on your phone.</v>

6
00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.130
<v Daniel>That sounds amazing, honestly.</v>

7
00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:48.377
<v Maya>I know, right? You just tell it what you need and it does all the tedious reading. It drafts these complex legal arguments, fills out the forms, waits on hold, and just, boom, hits submit.</v>

8
00:00:48.517 --> 00:00:51.579
<v Daniel>For a normal citizen, yeah, that sounds like an absolute dream come true.</v>

9
00:00:51.911 --> 00:01:06.006
<v Maya>But so here is the flip side. And this is what we are exploring today in this deep dive. What actually happens to the civil servants on the receiving end when suddenly, I mean, a million people can do exactly that all at the exact same time for basically zero cost?</v>

10
00:01:06.446 --> 00:01:09.950
<v Daniel>Well, the short answer is that the system just completely breaks.</v>

11
00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:25.079
<v Maya>Yeah. And the long answer is what we are digging into today. Our mission for this deep dive is to explore this brand new, frankly, groundbreaking 2026 paper by Chris Schmitz, Lewis Hammond and Alan Chan.</v>

12
00:01:25.399 --> 00:01:29.520
<v Daniel>Yeah, it's called Characterizing Agentic Flooding of Government Services. It's fascinating.</v>

13
00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:40.025
<v Maya>It really is. And we also have a stack of four older background papers that kind of explain the underlying bureaucratic theory here. But this new 2026 paper is the absolute star of the show.</v>

14
00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:47.701
<v Daniel>Definitely. And we should probably set the tone right away. This is not some story about, you know, futuristic sci-fi technology taking over the world. There are</v>

15
00:01:47.721 --> 00:01:48.561
<v Maya>no terminators here.</v>

16
00:01:48.681 --> 00:01:57.983
<v Daniel>Exactly. The tech driving this is already in literally everyone's pockets. It's a story about the really mundane, messy reality of civic institutions just colliding with everyday AI.</v>

17
00:01:58.403 --> 00:02:09.045
<v Maya>And the incredibly difficult choices that governments are being forced to make, like right now. So to start at the foundation, the authors use this very specific term, agentic flooding. How are they defining that?</v>

18
00:02:09.515 --> 00:02:20.321
<v Daniel>So they define agentic flooding as this massive surge in either the volume or, crucially, the complexity of requests that a government service receives.</v>

19
00:02:20.741 --> 00:02:22.422
<v Maya>Right. So it's not just more emails.</v>

20
00:02:22.582 --> 00:02:32.568
<v Daniel>No, no. It's a surge caused by AI agents that substantially strains the service. I mean, it fundamentally breaks a government office's capacity to actually process anything.</v>

21
00:02:32.891 --> 00:02:38.514
<v Maya>And their data set for this is just incredibly rigorous. I mean, they didn't just pull like a few random news anecdotes from Twitter.</v>

22
00:02:38.534 --> 00:02:47.900
<v Daniel>Yeah, they started with this massive funnel. I think it was 2,288 candidate services they looked at across 11 different jurisdictions and 13 service domains.</v>

23
00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:51.462
<v Maya>Which is roughly, what, 190 government services per country?</v>

24
00:02:51.502 --> 00:02:55.924
<v Daniel>Something like that, yeah. But they filtered that massive list down to just 84 verified cases.</v>

25
00:02:56.084 --> 00:03:02.428
<v Maya>Wow. So fewer than one in 20 candidate cases actually survived their filtering. Why were they so ruthless with the data?</v>

26
00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:11.121
<v Daniel>Because they wanted undeniable proof. And, you know, proving that a specific block of text was generated by AI is notoriously difficult.</v>

27
00:03:11.421 --> 00:03:15.006
<v Maya>Oh, because the automated AI detectors are basically garbage, right?</v>

28
00:03:15.412 --> 00:03:16.613
<v Daniel>Exactly. They're heavily flawed.</v>

29
00:03:16.733 --> 00:03:16.933
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

30
00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:28.802
<v Daniel>So the author set up these three incredibly strict inclusion criteria. First, there had to be a plausible, specific mechanism for how AI could even be used for that survey.</v>

31
00:03:28.822 --> 00:03:30.383
<v Maya>Okay. That makes sense. What's the second?</v>

32
00:03:30.523 --> 00:03:43.013
<v Daniel>Second, there had to be actual documented empirical evidence of a change in demand patterns. And third, and this is the one that knocked out most of the candidates, there had to be external attribution of that change to AI.</v>

33
00:03:43.718 --> 00:03:48.363
<v Maya>Meaning like someone officially on the record had to point the finger and say, AI did this.</v>

34
00:03:48.423 --> 00:03:56.451
<v Daniel>Yes. And what's really wild is that in 69% of those 84 cases, it was the government officials themselves asserting that AI was involved.</v>

35
00:03:56.471 --> 00:03:57.532
<v Maya>And the rest were third parties.</v>

36
00:03:57.792 --> 00:04:07.082
<v Daniel>Yeah. And the remaining 31%, it was reputable third parties. But because of how strict that third rule is, we really do have to be honest about the limits of this study so we don't oversell it.</v>

37
00:04:07.394 --> 00:04:12.056
<v Maya>Right. It's an exploratory, semi-automated, qualitative study.</v>

38
00:04:12.237 --> 00:04:24.403
<v Daniel>Exactly. They aren't making causal claims or claims about exactly how prevalent this is globally. I mean, only 25 of those cases even have actual time series volume data.</v>

39
00:04:24.913 --> 00:04:30.858
<v Maya>So because they require that public attribution, they're almost certainly undercounting the real phenomenon.</v>

40
00:04:31.098 --> 00:04:32.099
<v Daniel>Oh, 100 percent undercounting.</v>

41
00:04:32.339 --> 00:04:43.908
<v Maya>It's kind of like it's like we are only counting the meteors that actually leave a massive crater and also happen to have a government official standing right there pointing at the sky, taking a photo.</v>

42
00:04:44.327 --> 00:04:45.188
<v Daniel>That's a great way to put it.</v>

43
00:04:45.408 --> 00:04:50.912
<v Maya>We know there are way more out there, you know, burning up or hitting unpopulated areas. We just aren't counting them.</v>

44
00:04:51.092 --> 00:04:53.134
<v Daniel>Right. We're only looking at the undeniable craters.</v>

45
00:04:53.354 --> 00:04:56.256
<v Maya>So let's look at those craters. What do they actually look like in the real world?</v>

46
00:04:56.596 --> 00:05:04.442
<v Daniel>Well, let's take a global tour of the cases because it's pretty mind blowing. Germany is a prime example of what they call qualitative flooding.</v>

47
00:05:04.843 --> 00:05:09.047
<v Maya>Meaning the submissions aren't just more frequent, they are vastly more complex, right?</v>

48
00:05:09.347 --> 00:05:19.997
<v Daniel>Exactly. So in German public comment periods for environmental impact assessments, government agencies are suddenly getting these AI-generated letters that run over 4,000 pages long.</v>

49
00:05:20.177 --> 00:05:26.123
<v Maya>Wait, 4,000 pages? Why doesn't the government just, you know, throw a 4,000-page letter directly in the trash?</v>

50
00:05:26.788 --> 00:05:36.632
<v Daniel>Because legally, they can't. When they do an environmental impact assessment, they're legally mandated to review all public comments to ensure they haven't missed a unique, valid legal objection.</v>

51
00:05:36.852 --> 00:05:44.096
<v Maya>Oh, wow. So if a citizen uses AI to generate this massive, dense legal argument citing local statutes and all that.</v>

52
00:05:44.296 --> 00:05:54.320
<v Daniel>A human civil servant actually has to read it. If they just skim it and miss a legitimate objection buried on page 3211, the entire infrastructure project could get sued and halted.</v>

53
00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:59.013
<v Maya>That is just paralyzing. The human hours required for one synthetic document is insane.</v>

54
00:05:59.153 --> 00:06:07.619
<v Daniel>And it isn't just environmental stuff. The German social courts are blaming AI for a 55% year-on-year rise in their 2025 caseload.</v>

55
00:06:07.719 --> 00:06:17.165
<v Maya>And then in Australia, the volume of Federal Freedom of Information, or FOI, requests surged so much the government actually considered bringing fees back just to stem the tide.</v>

56
00:06:17.505 --> 00:06:24.010
<v Daniel>Yeah, because again, they're legally obligated to respond and produce documents, no matter how easy it was for someone to generate the request.</v>

57
00:06:24.568 --> 00:06:27.875
<v Maya>And there are financially motivated cases too, right? Like in the Netherlands.</v>

58
00:06:28.015 --> 00:06:34.468
