62
00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:09.042
<v Daniel>Exactly. And in Brazil, we see people using AI to submit bad faith medical certificates for temporary disability benefits.</v>

63
00:07:09.262 --> 00:07:17.765
<v Maya>Right. Shifting the burden of proof entirely to the state's medical reviewers. And didn't Japan have to block submissions by IP address for their strategic energy plan?</v>

64
00:07:18.185 --> 00:07:29.289
<v Daniel>Yeah, they were just getting hammered. And in the United States, the paper documents these organized adversarial campaigns using AI for voter challenge and voter roll purge submissions.</v>

65
00:07:29.825 --> 00:07:37.610
<v Maya>And just to be very clear to you listening, we are looking at this strictly from a mechanical perspective, totally neutral on the politics.</v>

66
00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:43.673
<v Daniel>Right. We're not validating or condemning the political motives there. It just illustrates the exact same vulnerability.</v>

67
00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:54.812
<v Maya>Right. AI allows any organized group to mass produce localized legal challenges that look entirely legitimate. And the local boards have to manually verify every single one.</v>

68
00:07:54.832 --> 00:08:06.715
<v Daniel>Yeah, the mechanics are identical. And this brings up a really important distinction the authors make. They split this into quantitative flooding, which is just more requests, and qualitative flooding, which is those 4,000 page letters.</v>

69
00:08:06.915 --> 00:08:08.075
<v Maya>And what were the numbers on that?</v>

70
00:08:08.672 --> 00:08:14.113
<v Daniel>60% of the cases are quantitative, 90% are qualitative, and about 50% involve both.</v>

71
00:08:14.493 --> 00:08:22.315
<v Maya>Okay, but when I was reading the methodology, I noticed something. They call this agentic flooding, but actual autonomous AI agents aren't really doing this, are they?</v>

72
00:08:22.595 --> 00:08:34.678
<v Daniel>That is such a crucial point. In 87% of these cases, the mechanism is totally mundane. It's just a person using an LLM, like ChatGPT, to generate legally sophisticated text cheaply.</v>

73
00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:37.713
<v Maya>And then they just manually copy, paste and click submit.</v>

74
00:08:37.933 --> 00:08:46.901
<v Daniel>Exactly. Autonomous agent web navigation, where the AI independently solves captios and submits the form, is not evident in the data yet.</v>

75
00:08:47.161 --> 00:08:59.933
<v Maya>Wait, so the systems aren't being overwhelmed by some autonomous digital masterminds hacking mainframes. They're being broken by people basically using a chat bot to write dense letters and hitting send manually.</v>

76
00:09:00.133 --> 00:09:12.164
<v Daniel>Yes. And you might wonder, why call it agentic then? It comes down to what is being delegated. The human is clicking, but the intellectual laborer reading the statutes structuring the argument, that agency is delegated to the AI.</v>

77
00:09:12.745 --> 00:09:19.832
<v Maya>Oh, okay. The bottleneck was never the physical act of clicking a mouse. It was the cognitive effort required to write a 20-page legal appeal.</v>

78
00:09:20.012 --> 00:09:26.438
<v Daniel>Exactly. AI automated the cognitive effort. And that brings us to the theoretical framework. Why are government systems so vulnerable to this?</v>

79
00:09:26.753 --> 00:09:33.644
<v Maya>Right. Let's bring in that 2024 paper by Holling and Baikgard. They define what they call the administrative burden framework.</v>

80
00:09:33.835 --> 00:09:46.722
<v Daniel>Yeah, their review of 119 articles shows that citizens don't just pay taxes, they pay in friction. Hauling a bait guard split this into three costs, learning costs, compliance costs, and psychological costs.</v>

81
00:09:46.802 --> 00:09:51.064
<v Maya>Learning the rules, doing the paperwork, and dealing with the stress of the bureaucracy.</v>

82
00:09:51.184 --> 00:10:02.950
<v Daniel>Right, and the new paper by Schmitz specifically maps AI's capabilities onto eliminating those exact three costs. AI learns the rules for you, drafts the documents for you, and shields you from the stress.</v>

83
00:10:03.322 --> 00:10:04.483
<v Maya>It just zeroes out the burden.</v>

84
00:10:04.543 --> 00:10:12.728
<v Daniel>Yeah. But crucially, not all services are equally at risk. The authors developed a 13-factor risk matrix to predict vulnerability.</v>

85
00:10:12.928 --> 00:10:13.909
<v Maya>How does that matrix work?</v>

86
00:10:14.349 --> 00:10:25.797
<v Daniel>They split risk into likelihood and severity. Likelihood looks at agent capability and the expected payoff of the service compared to the effort it takes. Severity looks at the operational impact and response impact.</v>

87
00:10:25.962 --> 00:10:28.564
<v Maya>And the conclusion they reach is pretty surprising, isn't it?</v>

88
00:10:28.804 --> 00:10:40.372
<v Daniel>It is. The highest risk isn't actually to basic digital forms. It's to financially attractive services, where demand was historically suppressed just by the sheer friction of the process.</v>

89
00:10:40.747 --> 00:10:42.649
<v Maya>Like tax administration and court systems.</v>

90
00:10:42.909 --> 00:10:43.390
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

91
00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:58.307
<v Maya>It's basically like a nightclub that never needed to hire a bouncer because the front door was made of solid lead and was almost impossible to push open. I love that. But now AI is handing everyone a forklift to open the door and the club is realizing they have absolutely no</v>

92
00:10:58.347 --> 00:10:59.268
<v Maya>crowd control inside.</v>

93
00:10:59.468 --> 00:11:04.773
<v Daniel>That is exactly what's happening. The door is wide open. The club is flooded. So what does the government actually do?</v>

94
00:11:05.054 --> 00:11:17.085
<v Maya>This is the most crucial part of this deep dive, really, the unresolvable tension here. And we have to bring in Richard Zeckhauser's 2019 economic theory paper, which is honestly a bit uncomfortable.</v>

95
00:11:17.265 --> 00:11:31.874
<v Daniel>It is uncomfortable because the burden literature, like Holling and Baingard, treats friction as a harm to be removed. But Zeckhauser argues that ordeals like meaningless waiting in complex forms are actually deliberate screening mechanisms.</v>

96
00:11:32.114 --> 00:11:35.434
<v Maya>Right. Because health and government resources are highly subsidized or free.</v>

97
00:11:35.794 --> 00:11:47.837
<v Daniel>Exactly. So to avoid just putting a price tag on them or setting hard quotas, the ordeal rations the scarce resources to those who value the most. Only the desperate will suffer through the paperwork.</v>

98
00:11:48.312 --> 00:11:57.715
<v Maya>That is so bleak, but it makes economic sense. And this connects to a concurrent 2026 theoretical paper by Piedrahita and a huge team of AI researchers.</v>

99
00:11:57.795 --> 00:12:09.998
<v Daniel>Yeah, including people like Yoshua Bengio. They predicted this exact system level risk from theory alone. They said AI removes the ordeal without replacing the screen, leading to a congested bureaucracy.</v>

100
00:12:10.338 --> 00:12:15.480
<v Maya>Which is exactly what our main paper proves with data. So what are the two ways governments are responding to this?</v>

101
00:12:15.973 --> 00:12:24.116
<v Daniel>Well, response one is suppress demand. They just put the friction back. They add fees, IP blocks, force in-person appointments, or add rate caps. And that</v>

102
00:12:24.156 --> 00:12:24.416
<v Maya>works.</v>

103
00:12:24.516 --> 00:12:35.681
<v Daniel>It's fast, it's cheap, and it's already been done in 14 of the 84 cases. But, and this is a massive but, we have to look at a 2025 paper by Sharpe and colleagues on agentic inequality.</v>

104
00:12:35.781 --> 00:12:39.002
<v Maya>Oh, and Hammond is an author on both that paper and our main paper, right?</v>

105
00:12:39.182 --> 00:12:54.085
<v Daniel>Yes, he is. And they argue that this response is wildly regressive. AI acts as an autonomous delegate. If governments add fees or complex hurdles to fight the AI, they instantly deter the poorest, least digitally literate citizens</v>

106
00:12:54.165 --> 00:12:54.465
<v Daniel>first.</v>

107
00:12:54.685 --> 00:12:59.106
<v Maya>So the fastest fix falls the hardest on the exact people AI was supposed to help.</v>

108
00:12:59.286 --> 00:13:09.626
<v Daniel>Exactly. Which brings us to response two, increase capacity. Redesign the systems, integrate digital ID, deploy backend AI to process the flood.</v>

109
00:13:09.906 --> 00:13:12.348
<v Maya>That avoids the inequality trade-off, surely.</v>

110
00:13:12.488 --> 00:13:20.813
<v Daniel>It does, but it requires years of lead time and massive budgets. That is entirely useless when a surge of 4,000-page complaints is hitting you today.</v>

111
00:13:21.113 --> 00:13:32.435
